### From print statements to events

**Concrete starting point.** This is the Brewline checkout handler as many teams first write it:

```javascript
console.log("Received order");
console.log("Order " + order.id + " for user " + user.id);
console.log("Charging card...");
console.log("Order " + order.id + " failed for user " + user.id + " after " + ms + "ms: " + err);
```

Each line is a sentence written for a human reading one request at a time. In production you are never reading one request at a time. You are asking questions across thousands of them, such as "how many failed?", "which stores?" and "how slow?". Sentences can't answer those questions without fragile text parsing.

function logEvent(level, event, fields) {
  const line = { ts: "2026-09-21T10:15:02.114Z", level, event, ...fields };
  console.log(JSON.stringify(line));
}
// Before: console.log("Order 8812 failed for user 42 after 312ms: card declined");
logEvent("warn", "order.payment_declined", {
  requestId: "req-7f3a", orderId: 8812, userId: 42,
  durationMs: 312, outcome: "declined", reason: "card_declined",
});

